WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.259
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by the CuriosityStream &amp; Nebula bundle

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:10.660
deal. Watch until the end of the video to
see a trailer for our upcoming Nebula Original

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:23.949
series, The Battle of Britain.

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:35.380
For 
the first time in human history, a spacecraft

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:40.430
has flown through the atmosphere of the Sun.
Sweeping through the super heated particles

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:45.670
of the Corona. A momentous moment that has
provided scientists vital information on the

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:50.890
nature of our closest star, that may help
us unlock its mysteries.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:55.330
The Sun’s atmosphere, much like our own
atmosphere, is composed of layers. Where we

00:00:55.330 --> 00:01:00.960
have the troposphere, stratosphere and mesosphere,
the sun has the photosphere, the chromosphere

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.420
and the corona. [1]

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.680
We have a saying: hotter than the surface
of the Sun, but the surface of the sun, the

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.910
photosphere, is not actually that hot.It ranges
from about 6200 degrees celsius (6500 Kelvin)

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:17.900
at the bottom and 3700 degrees celsius (4000
Kelvin) at the top.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.860
That’s about the same temperature as a welding
arc [2], and the air around a lightning bolt

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.620
can reach temperatures 5 times hotter than
the photosphere. [3]

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.159
What’s weird, is that the outermost layer
of the Sun’s atmosphere, the corona, is

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:36.930
much much hotter than the photosphere. The
corona, starting about 2100 kilometers above

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:42.740
the surface of the sun, reaches half a million
degrees celsius, 80 times higher than the

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.740
surface.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.560
That’s like walking away from a fire and
the temperature getting warmer as you get

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:49.620
further away from it.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.729
This is a strange anomaly that makes the Parker
Solar Probe’s achievement even more impressive,

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.960
as at first glance it’s easy to dismiss
that the probe “only” flew through the

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.330
upper atmosphere [4], when in reality the
upper atmosphere is vastly hotter than the

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:11.730
surface. The reason this occurs is one of
the many unsolved mysteries of the universe,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:17.870
and as such, is one of the primary missions
of Parker Solar Probe. To gather information

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:23.370
on the magnetic fields and charged particles
in this region, and attempt to answer this

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.510
riddle.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:29.180
To understand the achievement of the Parker
Solar Probe, let’s dive into the engineering

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:35.200
and physics of its solar mission. The first
big problem facing the Parker Solar is actually

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.370
reaching the Sun. Despite the Sun’s gravity
acting as the anchor for our entire solar

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.570
system, getting close to it is not easy.

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:49.160
In order to bring a satellite out of orbit
around the Earth, we have to shed its angular

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.280
momentum so that it falls back to Earth. The
same has to be done when trying to bring something

00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:03.130
out of orbit around the Sun, except in this
case, we are located 1 AU, or 150 million

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:08.569
kilometers from the Sun, and traveling at
30 kilometers per second [5]. And anything

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:14.300
launching from Earth will be imbued with the
same orbital velocity around the Sun.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:19.569
This means in order to achieve a tighter orbit
to the sun we need to lower the spacecraft's

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:25.710
orbital velocity around the Sun, and this
is an incredibly energy intensive task, especially

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.030
when you add the energy needed to escape earth’s
gravity.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.989
So, let’s say we want to first get our satellite
from the Earth's surface into orbit around

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:39.520
Earth, this will require us to accelerate
our satellite by around 9.2 km/s kilometers

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.850
per second relative to Earth’s surface.
The satellite is now in orbit around earth,

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:50.599
and traveling with it at 30 kilometers per
second around the Sun.

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:55.879
From here we need to perform something called
a Hohmann transfer. This is an orbital maneuver

00:03:55.879 --> 00:04:00.340
where we either change the spacecraft’s
orbital energy to adjust it’s perihelion,

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:06.080
its closest approach to the Sun, or it’s
aphelion, its furthest approach from the Sun.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.830
To visit an outer planet, like Mars, we want
to increase our aphelion by adding to the

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:16.500
spacecraft’s orbital energy. While reaching
an inner planet requires us to reduce our

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.289
perihelion by decreasing our orbital energy.
[6]

00:04:20.289 --> 00:04:26.669
To reach Mars from earth’s orbit requires
a delta v of around 2.9 kilometers per second.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:32.249
To reach Venus requires around 2.5 kilometers
per second. This is found using this equation:

00:04:32.249 --> 00:04:37.909
Where mu, this greek letter that looks like
a u, is the planetary parameter of the sun,

00:04:37.909 --> 00:04:42.939
which is a product of the Sun’s mass. R1
is the orbital radius we are starting from,

00:04:42.939 --> 00:04:49.909
in this case Earth’s distance from the sun
at 150 million kilometers, and r2 is the desired

00:04:49.909 --> 00:04:56.189
perihelion or aphelion. If we calculate the
delta v required to reach the solar parker

00:04:56.189 --> 00:05:05.789
probe's closest approach of 6.2 million kilometers.
This would require a delta V of 21.4, over

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:10.150
8.5 times greater than the delta V required
to reach Venus.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:16.979
That’s an incredibly high delta V. One beyond
the capabilities of any rocket ever made.

00:05:16.979 --> 00:05:23.660
But, on December 8th 2018, the Parker space
probe launched from Cape Canaveral aboard

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.710
the Delta IV heavy, the world’s second highest
capacity rocket in the world, second only

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.460
to the Falcon Heavy. [7]

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.809
To give the probe an extra push, the Delta
IV was fitted with a special solid rocket

00:05:35.809 --> 00:05:43.529
third stage, providing an additional 3 km/s
of delta v for the typically two staged rocket

00:05:43.529 --> 00:05:44.529
design. [8]

00:05:44.529 --> 00:05:49.469
Yet, even with this added power, the probe
would never have gotten close to the Sun.

00:05:49.469 --> 00:05:54.379
To achieve it’s record breaking flight,
one seventh that of the previous record hold

00:05:54.379 --> 00:06:01.430
of Helios 2, the Parker Solar Probe completed
an astounding 5 gravity assists by Venus with

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:06.479
an additional 2 flybys due in 2023 and 2024.

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:11.879
This number of flybys was needed because Venus
is a relatively low mass planet.

00:06:11.879 --> 00:06:17.539
The magnitude of velocity change a planet
can provide is largely determined by its gravity,

00:06:17.539 --> 00:06:24.129
determined by its mass. In fact, the original
plan for the probe was to do a single planetary

00:06:24.129 --> 00:06:30.521
assist by Jupiter, which would have brought
the probe 3 times closer to the Sun, but this

00:06:30.521 --> 00:06:33.749
trajectory came with some issues. [9]

00:06:33.749 --> 00:06:38.779
Because Jupiter's orbit is so much further
from the sun, the sunlight reaching the panels

00:06:38.779 --> 00:06:45.240
at its aphelion would have been 25 times dimmer,
requiring much larger solar panels to power

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.899
the spacecraft. This poses an issue when the
spacecraft makes its way around Jupiter and

00:06:50.899 --> 00:06:56.810
begins to accelerate towards the Sun. The
heat of the Sun would destroy the solar panels,

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:02.909
and at this size they could not retract and
hide behind the sunshield. Other options were

00:07:02.909 --> 00:07:08.599
available. A radioisotope thermal generator
could have been used, but would have driven

00:07:08.599 --> 00:07:13.419
the cost, weight and complexity of the spacecraft
up significantly.

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:18.839
The real selling point for this radical new
flight trajectory was the added time and data

00:07:18.839 --> 00:07:23.789
it would provide scientists to fulfill the
probe's mission, to study the Sun. With the

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:29.210
original Jupiter plan the probe would have
had just 100 hours inside the desired zone

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:35.069
around the Sun, completing just 2 solar passes
before the probe reached the end of it’s

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:36.449
8 year mission duration.

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:41.520
While the new lower orbit plan would mean
the Parker solar probe would take less than

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:47.669
150 days to complete its orbit around the
Sun, allowing scientists to gather over 900

00:07:47.669 --> 00:07:52.550
hours worth of data over the probe's 24 orbits
around the Sun.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:57.639
The change in plan came be with a change of
design, moving away from the original conical

00:07:57.639 --> 00:08:02.699
shaped heat shield [10] to the familiar and
compact flat shield. This shield is primarily

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:09.169
constructed from 11.4 centimeter carbon foam.
A truly fascinating material developed by

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:13.599
one of the most prolific material innovation
labs, Ultramet. [11]

00:08:13.599 --> 00:08:19.379
Under a scanning electron microscope the carbon
foam looks like this. An incredibly porous

00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:25.969
material, dominated by open space, making
its internal volume 97% empty space, providing

00:08:25.969 --> 00:08:31.220
the heat shield fantastic insulation properties,
while also benefiting from carbon's thermal

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.430
stability. [12]

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:38.060
Next a carbon carbon composite, which is made
by combining graphite with an organic binder,

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:43.760
such as pitch or an epoxy resin. This mixture
was applied to each side of the foam, before

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:49.890
being super heated to transform the binder
into a pure form of carbon. Creating a carbon-carbon

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.180
composite.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:56.450
Finally a white ceramic paint was applied
to the Sun facing side to reflect as much

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:01.740
of that heat away from the heat shield before
it had a chance to even enter the labyrinth

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.510
of carbon beneath.

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:08.790
From here the rest of the spacecraft, besides
a few specialized sensors and solar panels,

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:13.530
had to be designed to fit within the umbra,
or shadow, of the shield.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:18.280
There are several instruments that bravely
peak out beyond the safety of the heat shield.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:23.840
Like the solar probe cup, one of the many
sensors on board. This thing is easily the

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:29.290
most impressive bit of technology aboard.
Completely unprotected by the sunshield, its

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:33.090
designers had to get very creative with materials.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:37.600
The solar probe cup is a faraday cup, which
is a device that can count and measure the

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.810
properties of electrons and ions coming from
the Sun, essentially giving the spacecraft

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:48.280
the capability of studying solar winds and
mass ejections of particles coming from the

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:49.280
Sun.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:54.460
This is the cross section of the solar probe
cup. It essentially works by applying an electric

00:09:54.460 --> 00:10:00.380
field over the grid at the cup's opening.
By varying the voltage, we can select or filter

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:06.120
out particles that can enter the cup, giving
us more data on what is causing current as

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:11.650
these charged particles strike the collecting
plate at the bottom of the cup. It’s a very

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:18.630
simple device in practice, but with the temperatures
it’s facing, 1400 degrees celsius, just

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:25.040
below the melting point of pure iron, the
solar probe cup needed some innovative engineering.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.600
The first challenge was selecting a material
for the electric grid that generates the selecting

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:35.020
electric field at the entrance of the cup.
This grid needed to be conductive and heat

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:41.700
resistant, while also being machinable to
create the tiny 100 micron spaced grid.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.700
Tungsten was chosen, the same material used
in incandescent light bulbs here on earth,

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:52.280
as they are capable of surviving the extremely
high temperatures needed to generate light.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:58.100
Tungsten light filaments operate at temperatures
as 3000 degrees celsius [13], so are more

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:03.230
than capable of surviving these temperatures,
however, machining tungsten into a grid this

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:05.570
fine is difficult.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:10.220
Micron scale machining like this is not done
with traditional tools. You would immediately

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:15.870
break the grid with the force required to
shave the metal away. Instead lasers are typically

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:22.280
used to etch away material, but because tungsten
is so heat resistant, lasers would not be

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:28.740
capable of melting the tungsten to form the
grid. Instead acid etching was used. [14]

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:33.720
Next we needed electrical cables capable of
supplying the grid with power, and carrying

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:38.790
electrical signals away from the collecting
plate. The two most commonly used conductors

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:45.140
here on earth, copper and aluminum, would
turn to a pool of molten metal in these conditions,

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:48.030
so these were most definitely not an option.

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:54.240
Any conducting cables in this part of the
spacecraft had to be made out of niobium C-103,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:01.510
a special alloy of 89% niobium 10% hafnium
and 1% titanium. All the external casing components

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:07.100
were also constructed from this exotic aerospace
material. Normally wires would be insulated

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:11.780
from outer casings with plastic, but this
obviously wasn’t an option for the Parker

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:17.430
Space Probe, and the engineers were forced
to use sapphire to ensure the niobium wires

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:21.160
were insulated.[15]
These are some extremely exotic materials

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:25.550
to perform what is a relatively mundane job
here on earth.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:31.060
Other portions of sensors peaking beyond the
sunshield are built in a similar manner. The

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:36.100
magnetic field measuring instruments hidden
behind the shield need antennas that reach

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:41.660
beyond the sunshield in order to make it’s
measurements of the Sun’s magnetic fields.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:46.060
These 4 antennas are also made from niobium
C-103. [16]

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:50.120
The solar panels were the next challenge.
While cruising through it’s distant orbit

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:55.660
around the Sun, the spacecraft can fully deploy
it’s solar panels without issue, but as

00:12:55.660 --> 00:13:02.130
the probe begins its sweep towards the Sun
heat will become an ever growing issue. This

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:07.350
can be counteracted somewhat by retracting
the solar panels, but the spacecraft needs

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:13.740
to maintain some power to operate it’s scientific
equipment during this vital stage of flight.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:20.400
Here two smaller secondary panels remain sticking
out in view of the Sun and are cooled with

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:26.000
water, which is pumped through the solar panels
and into these black radiators attached to

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.240
the titanium truss just below the sunshield.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:34.370
This truss is exceptionally light for how
big it looks. The entire truss only weighs

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:40.430
22.7 kilograms (50 pounds) [17] , which even
for low density titanium is very low for the

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:46.330
size of the truss. The engineers at NASA have
clearly triple checked their stress calculations

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:52.190
to ensure this thing could use as little material
as possible, which of course saves launch

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:58.740
weight, but also minimizes the material available
to conduct heat from the heat shield to the

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.110
spacecraft bus.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:04.460
Testing these systems in the heat they are
expected to meet is difficult on earth. The

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:10.020
Odeillo Solar Furnace is our best approximation
of the environment they would need to endure.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:16.380
This facility, built on a hillside in rural
France, uses 10,000 adjustable mirrors to

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:22.920
focus light onto one concave mirror. The facility
is capable of reaching temperatures as high

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:29.610
as 3500 degrees celsius, over double the temperature
that even the sunshield will experience. Parts

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:34.690
like the faraday cup and sunshield were placed
in the focal point of this concave mirror

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:39.790
and exposed to the temperatures they will
need to endure. However, components like the

00:14:39.790 --> 00:14:45.340
faraday cup also needed to be tested while
performing their sensory tasks, and for this

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:50.710
the engineers need a particle accelerator
to simulate the electrons and ions it will

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:56.610
encounter from solar wind. Combining a particle
accelerator with this solar furnace was not

00:14:56.610 --> 00:15:02.050
an option, so the researchers at the University
of Michigan came up with the bright idea of

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:08.960
using 4 high powered IMAX projectors to simulate
the heat of the sun, and they found that the

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:15.680
faraday cup actually performs better when
heated, as the heat decontaminates the system.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.980
Much of the data we have received from these
instruments is of little interest to the average

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:25.529
space enthusiast. Raw data that will provide
scientists with valuable clues to the nature

00:15:25.529 --> 00:15:31.310
of the Sun, but there is one sensor on board
relaying images back to Earth that we can

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:32.970
all enjoy.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:38.770
During a solar eclipse we can observe a beautiful
phenomenon, bright loops of light dancing

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:44.520
around the Sun. These fantastic patterns are
created by glowing electrons, sailing around

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:50.190
the Sun on magnetic field lines, distorted
by solar winds. [18] We have been able to

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:55.960
observe the streams of high energy electrons
from Earth and our solar observatories parked

00:15:55.960 --> 00:16:01.830
in Lagrange point 1, but we have never, until
very recently, been able to observe them from

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:03.370
up close.

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:08.570
As the Parker Space Probe dipped into the
Corona for its ninth encounter with the Sun,

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:14.730
it began recording from its wideview imager,
here on the spacecraft. The images it delivered

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:21.220
look like a passenger's view of a passing
snowstorm on a dark night. Bright sub-particles

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:26.550
streaming by the probe as it dips into the
eye of the storm. Beautiful images that no

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:33.500
doubt are giving scientists unparalleled data
on the nature of the coronal streamers.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:38.310
The Parker Space Probe has many more rendezvous
with the Sun, with the next due in September

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:45.270
of 2022, and with another 2 Venus flybys the
Parker Space Probe will be breaking its own

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:51.089
records in 2023 and 2024, bringing us even
closer to the Sun.

00:16:51.089 --> 00:16:52.089
[19]

00:16:52.089 --> 00:16:55.790
And while we are getting closer to the Sun
in our skies, we are also getting closer to

00:16:55.790 --> 00:17:01.670
recreating the Sun here on earth for boundless
fusion power. The current record for fusion

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:08.429
power is setting at 70%, meaning we have managed
to reclaim 70% of the power needed to get

00:17:08.429 --> 00:17:14.329
the fusion reactor started. We are still well
away from being able to actually create power

00:17:14.329 --> 00:17:21.539
using fusion, but ITER, a new Fusion reactor
is due to be complete in 2025, with worldwide

00:17:21.539 --> 00:17:28.069
cooperation helping to fund its astronomical
cost. Upwards of 45 billion dollars, making

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:32.490
it one of the world’s most expensive science
experiments in human history.

